{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a6c6fc60-18bf-475d-8e42-0e2d87d85ff2","step":{"id":"s1","script":"We approximate the area under y equals x plus one, from zero to three, using three rectangles of width one.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"C1","content":"Area by rectangles"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"f(x)=x+1,\\quad 0\\leq x\\leq 3"},{"op":"diagram","id":"area","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.15,0.85],[0.93,0.85]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true,"label":"x"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.15,0.85],[0.15,0.12]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true,"label":"y"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.15,0.7],[0.84,0.25]],"stroke":"solid","label":"y = x + 1"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.15,0.91],"content":"0"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.38,0.91],"content":"1"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.61,0.91],"content":"2"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.84,0.91],"content":"3"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.09,0.7],"content":"1"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.09,0.55],"content":"2"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.09,0.4],"content":"3"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.09,0.25],"content":"4"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a6c6fc60-18bf-475d-8e42-0e2d87d85ff2","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Use each interval’s left endpoint for its height. The first rectangle has height one, so its area is one.","ops":[{"op":"line","id":"firsttop","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.15,0.7],"to":[0.38,0.7],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"line","id":"firstside","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.38,0.7],"to":[0.38,0.85],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"equation","id":"width","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\Delta x=1,\\qquad A_1=1\\cdot1=1"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a6c6fc60-18bf-475d-8e42-0e2d87d85ff2","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The second rectangle uses the height at x equals one: two. Since the function rises, these left-endpoint rectangles lie below it.","ops":[{"op":"line","id":"secondleft","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.38,0.85],"to":[0.38,0.55],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"line","id":"secondtop","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.38,0.55],"to":[0.61,0.55],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"line","id":"secondright","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.61,0.55],"to":[0.61,0.85],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"equation","id":"secondarea","anchor":{"el":"width","side":"below","gap":0.07},"latex":"A_2=2\\cdot1=2"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What height should the third left-endpoint rectangle have?","expected_gist":"Three, because its left endpoint is x = 2 and f(2) = 3."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a6c6fc60-18bf-475d-8e42-0e2d87d85ff2","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The third height is three. Adding height times width gives six square units, an underestimate. Narrower rectangles improve the approximation.","ops":[{"op":"line","id":"thirdleft","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.61,0.85],"to":[0.61,0.4],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"line","id":"thirdtop","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.61,0.4],"to":[0.84,0.4],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"line","id":"thirdright","canvas_id":"area","from":[0.84,0.4],"to":[0.84,0.85],"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"equation","id":"sum","anchor":{"el":"secondarea","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"L_3=\\sum_{i=0}^{2}f(i)\\Delta x=(1+2+3)\\cdot1=6"}],"checkpoint":null}}
